(3654096, 509407, 60174, '<td valign="top">Ok, so for the record, yes, the person in the blue dress on the left is male... he''s the ref (what a good sport to wear a dress too, huh?). And yes, they wear ridiculous makeup on purpose.  <br/> <br/>Those of you wondering why:<br/>The point is to raise awareness about the sexualization and stigmatization that women in sports (especially contact sports) experience.  Rugby is still seen largely as a man''s sport, so playing Rugby in prom dresses is meant to be an ironic juxtaposition of feminine prom dresses with a sport generally seen as very masculine.  A lot of women''s rugby clubs across the US (and maybe in other contries too?) have prom dress games every year.  Many of them raise money to benefit women''s charities.  Aside from all that, it''s just a lot of fun for both spectators and players.  This is without fail our best attended game every year.</td>', 'Ok, so for the record, yes, the person in the blue dress on the left is male... he''s the ref (what a good sport to wear a dress too, huh?). And yes, they wear ridiculous makeup on purpose.   Those of you wondering why:The point is to raise awareness about the sexualization and stigmatization that women in sports (especially contact sports) experience.  Rugby is still seen largely as a man''s sport, so playing Rugby in prom dresses is meant to be an ironic juxtaposition of feminine prom dresses with a sport generally seen as very masculine.  A lot of women''s rugby clubs across the US (and maybe in other contries too?) have prom dress games every year.  Many of them raise money to benefit women''s charities.  Aside from all that, it''s just a lot of fun for both spectators and players.  This is without fail our best attended game every year.', '2007-05-16 02:29:51.000000', NULL, 0),
