(3659717, 508613, 44825, '<td valign="top">I thank you all for your comments which with one noticeable exception - (I had no idea my camera had a clutch!)- I found both useful and encouraging.<br/> <br/>Most of you made reference to the darkness of the image and in my defence I plead that it was in fact a dark and mostly horrible day! I appreciate that I could have played with the exposure settings on RAW conversion but I am new to this game and didn''t want to transgress the basic editing rules. I will try harder next time.<br/></td>', 'I thank you all for your comments which with one noticeable exception - (I had no idea my camera had a clutch!)- I found both useful and encouraging. Most of you made reference to the darkness of the image and in my defence I plead that it was in fact a dark and mostly horrible day! I appreciate that I could have played with the exposure settings on RAW conversion but I am new to this game and didn''t want to transgress the basic editing rules. I will try harder next time.', '2007-05-17 11:07:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(3659730, 508047, 47797, '<td valign="top">Thanks for the update - I can see a few of the ''hard edged'' areas now - under the armpit and where the jersey tucks into the pants seem to be the most revealing - I wonder if this is being caused by the limited color gamut of your color mode in-camera (standard RGB)? Are you converting to Adobe RGB in processing? Try setting your camera to AdobeRGB and keep that profile throughout your workflow - <br/> <br/>My wife has a D50 and we only shoot in aRGB RAW, however, when we first bought the camera, she (unknowingly) was using the default setting of sRGB and it sure seemed like we had a lot of quirky little color problems similar to what you experienced above ... in addition to how much more vibrant aRGB is, I would suggest giving it a shot if you are able to consistently reproduce the problem that got your pic disqualified. <br/> <br/>Good Luck and let me know how it goes,<br/>Jason</td>', 'Thanks for the update - I can see a few of the ''hard edged'' areas now - under the armpit and where the jersey tucks into the pants seem to be the most revealing - I wonder if this is being caused by the limited color gamut of your color mode in-camera (standard RGB)? Are you converting to Adobe RGB in processing? Try setting your camera to AdobeRGB and keep that profile throughout your workflow -  My wife has a D50 and we only shoot in aRGB RAW, however, when we first bought the camera, she (unknowingly) was using the default setting of sRGB and it sure seemed like we had a lot of quirky little color problems similar to what you experienced above ... in addition to how much more vibrant aRGB is, I would suggest giving it a shot if you are able to consistently reproduce the problem that got your pic disqualified.  Good Luck and let me know how it goes,Jason', '2007-05-17 11:12:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(3659836, 509171, 24454, '<td valign="top">What I love about this version is the closer-up feel you get to the runners.    And looking up at them makes them look strong and determined.  There''s nothing at all distracting in the photo -- just the team of runners -- and a couple of points of interest.   As I always think about your photos that end up lower than a kick-butt score -- it looks to me like one that should have been in the mid-6''s!<br/> <br/></td>', 'What I love about this version is the closer-up feel you get to the runners.    And looking up at them makes them look strong and determined.  There''s nothing at all distracting in the photo -- just the team of runners -- and a couple of points of interest.   As I always think about your photos that end up lower than a kick-butt score -- it looks to me like one that should have been in the mid-6''s! ', '2007-05-17 11:50:40.000000', NULL, 0),
